> Simple examples

A four-sample impulse at index one with a rectangular window has complex bins -i at k=1 and -1 at k=2. Hann windowing halves its amplitude.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/audio_features_impulse.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n=dm::nn;
  const auto x=n::tensor({0,1,0,0},{4},n::DType::Float64);
  const auto rectangular=n::stft(x,4,4,4,n::ones({4},n::DType::Float64));
  const auto hann=n::stft(x,4,4,4);
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.lines({0,1,2},n::power_spectrogram(rectangular).to_vector<double>(),"rectangle",{37,99,235})
   .lines({0,1,2},n::power_spectrogram(hann).to_vector<double>(),"Hann",{234,88,12})
   .title("One impulse, two windows").x_label("FFT bin").y_label("power").y_limits(0,1.3);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(800,420).save_svg("build/audio_features_impulse.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("bin",std::vector<double>{0,1,2});
  table.add_column("real",rectangular.real.to_vector<double>());
  table.add_column("imag",rectangular.imag.to_vector<double>());
  table.add_column("Hann_power",n::power_spectrogram(hann).to_vector<double>());
  std::print("{}\n",table.to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| bin | real | imag | Hann_power |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1 | 0 | 0.25 |
| 1 | 0 | -1 | 0.25 |
| 2 | -1 | 0 | 0.25 |

\image(figures/audio_features_impulse.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Window gain changes power before mel aggregation.)
\alttext(Window gain changes power before mel aggregation.)

> Checking the theory

A periodic 1 kHz sine sampled at 8 kHz lies at bin 32 of a 256-point FFT. The Hann peak amplitude is A L/4 = 24 for A=.375. Doubling N moves the same peak to bin 64.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/audio_features_padding.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n=dm::nn;
  const auto x=n::sin(n::arange(0,2048,1,n::DType::Float64)*(2*std::numbers::pi*1000/8000))*.375;
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table; std::vector<double> lengths,frames,peak,energy,error;
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for(int size:{256,512}) {
    const auto spectrum=n::stft(x,256,128,size);
    const auto power=n::power_spectrogram(spectrum);
    const auto row=n::take_rows(power,{0}).to_vector<double>();
    std::vector<double> hz, plotted; double sum=0; size_t best=0;
    for(size_t k=0;k<row.size();++k) {
      if(k*8000./size>=850 && k*8000./size<=1150){hz.push_back(k*8000./size);plotted.push_back(row[k]);} sum+=row[k]*(k==0||k==row.size()-1?1:2)/size;
      if(row[k]>row[best]) best=k;
    }
    const auto windowed=n::slice_axis(x,0,0,256)*n::hann_window(256,true,n::DType::Float64);
    const double expected=n::sum(n::square(windowed)).item();
    if(std::abs(sum-expected)>1e-9 || best*8000./size!=1000) return 1;
    lengths.push_back(size);frames.push_back(power.size(0));peak.push_back(best);energy.push_back(sum);error.push_back(std::abs(sum-expected));
    p.lines(hz,plotted,std::to_string(size)+" point FFT",size==256?dm::graphics::RGB{37,99,235}:dm::graphics::RGB{234,88,12});
  }
  p.title("Zero-padding samples the same spectral lobe").x_label("Hz").y_label("power");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build"); p.size(850,430).save_svg("build/audio_features_padding.svg");
  table.add_column("FFT_length",lengths);table.add_column("frames",frames);table.add_column("peak_bin",peak);
  table.add_column("Parseval_energy",energy);table.add_column("absolute_error",error);std::print("{}\n",table.to_md(9));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| FFT_length | frames | peak_bin | Parseval_energy | absolute_error |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 256 | 15 | 32 | 6.75 | 0 |
| 512 | 15 | 64 | 6.75 | 0 |

\image(figures/audio_features_padding.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Both FFT grids locate the same 1 kHz tone; one-sided Parseval recovers window energy.)
\alttext(Both FFT grids locate the same 1 kHz tone; one-sided Parseval recovers window energy.)

>> Storage and the silence floor

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n=dm::nn;
  const auto samples=n::tensor({-.5,-.1,0,.1,.5},{5,1});
  const auto wave=n::decode_wav(n::encode_wav(samples,8000));
  const auto weights=n::linear_to_mel_weight_matrix(20,129,8000,0,4000);
  const auto silent=n::log_mel_spectrogram(n::power_spectrogram(n::stft(n::zeros({256}),256,128,256)),weights);
  const double error=n::max(n::abs(wave.samples-samples)).item();
  if(error>1./32768 || std::abs(n::mean(silent).item()-std::log(1e-6))>2e-6) return 1;
  std::print("PCM16 maximum error: {:.9f}; sample rate: {} Hz.\n\nSilence log-mel mean: {:.6f}; short-signal frames: {}.\n",
    error,wave.sample_rate,n::mean(silent).item(),n::stft(n::zeros({10}),256,128,256).real.size(0));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
PCM16 maximum error: 0.000006102; sample rate: 8000 Hz.

Silence log-mel mean: -13.815511; short-signal frames: 0.
// result_end


> Conclusion

The impulse makes the window's gain explicit. The tone checks both the bin-frequency mapping
and the one-sided energy convention, while silence remains finite. These features discard
phase and depend on window, hop, band edges and log floor; they do not by themselves recognise sound.

> References

- [TensorFlow signal STFT conventions](https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/signal/stft).
- [TensorFlow mel weight matrix](https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/signal/linear_to_mel_weight_matrix).
